  "slug": "disorder-chern-plateaus-7d05",
  "summary": "Recent preprints move transport modeling of Chern, orbital Hall, and Weyl systems beyond clean-limit and constant-relaxation-time treatments.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that transport in topological and orbital-active systems is being recast with explicit disorder rather than clean-limit or constant-relaxation-time descriptions. In twisted bilayer MoTe2, layer-dependent disorder is reported to shape the widths and transitions of integer and fractional Chern plateaus. A Kubo-Bastin Chebyshev study of a centrosymmetric sp3 square lattice examines whether the quadrupole-origin orbital Hall effect survives disorder and how it can be tuned. For multi-Weyl semimetals, a generalized semiclassical Boltzmann treatment beyond constant relaxation time incorporates orbital moment and intervalley scattering, with monopole charge N as a parameter. The newest item extends the picture: structural and electrostatic disorder, including in amorphous graphene, is reported to generate sizable orbital Hall currents absent in pristine systems. Together these works treat disorder as a source of transport response rather than a perturbation to be neglected.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-20"
}
